      "math_academy_id": "q-213433",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={2,4,8}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,4),(2,8),(4,2),(8,2)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\n \nthe relation is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${2,4,8}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(2,4)\\in R$ and $(4,2)\\in R$\n\n- $(2,8)\\in R$ and $(8,2)\\in R$\n\nThere are no other pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ in the relation. So, for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the relation is  $\\text{symmetric}.$",
